We use the following statement for linear isomorphisms of Banach spaces.  egin{thm}[{\cite[Theorem 1]{NilsonPeris}}] For any invertible continuous linear operator $T$ on any Banach space $X$, if $T$ has the finite Lipschitz shadowing property with constant $L$, then $T$ has the Lipschitz shadowing property with constant $2L$. \end{thm}  egin{remark} The statement of Theorem 1 in \cite{NilsonPeris} gives only the shadowing property without specifying the constant. However, the proof can be repeated to establish the constant $2L$. For completeness of exposition we provide the proof tracing the constant in Appendix A. \end{remark} Consider the Banach space$ \mathfrak{X} =\ell^{\infty}(\mathbb{Z}, \mathbb{B})= igl\{\{v_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}: v_n\in \mathbb{B}  igr\} $ and a linear operator $\mathfrak{A} \colon \mathfrak{X} 	o \mathfrak{X}$ defined by $$ \mathfrak{A}\{v_n\}=\{v_n'\}, \qquad 	ext{where }\; v_{n+1}'=A_n v_n. $$ Let us prove that under the assumptions of Lemma ef{lem:Bk-Inf}, the operator $\mathfrak{A}$ has the finite shadowing property. Consider a finite interval $[k_1, k_2]$ and sequences $\xi^k\in \mathfrak{X}$, $k \in [k_1, k_2]$, with $|\xi^k|\leq1$; denote by $\xi_n^k \in \mathbb{B}$ ($k\in[k_1,k_2]$, $n\in\mathbb{Z}$) their elements. Below we show that there exists a sequence $
u^k \in \mathfrak{X}$, $k\in[k_1,k_2]$ with $|
u^k| \leq L$ such that  egin{equation} 
u^{k+1}=\mathfrak{A}
u^k+\xi^k,\quad k \in [k_1, k_2-1]. \end{equation} Denote the elements of $
u^k$ by $
u^k_n \in \mathbb{B}$, i.e. $
u^k = \{
u^k_n\}$. Equations \eqref{eq:nu} are equivalent to  egin{equation} 
u_{n+1}^{k+1}=A_n 
u_n^k+\xi_{n+1}^k, \qquad n \in \mathbb{Z},\; k \in [k_1,k_2-1]. \end{equation} The idea is to read the doubly-indexed system \eqref{eq:1-inf} along the diagonals $k-n=j$: on each of them it becomes a finite inhomogeneous difference equation in the single index $n$ for the operators $\{A_n\}$, to which the finite bounded solution property applies. Denote $j := k-n$ and consider the sequences  egin{equation} \mu_n^j := 
u_n^{j+n},\qquad 	heta_n^j := \xi_n^{j+n-1}. \end{equation} Then \eqref{eq:1-inf} is equivalent to  egin{equation} \mu_{n+1}^j = A_n\mu_n^j+	heta_{n+1}^j,\qquad n\in\mathbb{Z},\; n+j\in[k_1,k_2-1]. \end{equation} Rewriting indexes we have  egin{equation} \mu_{n+1}^j = A_n\mu_n^j+	heta_{n+1}^j,\qquad j\in\mathbb{Z},\; n\in[k_1-j,\,k_2-j-1]. \end{equation} For any $j\in\mathbb{Z}$, according to \eqref{eq:Inh} there exists a sequence $\mu^j_n$ satisfying \eqref{eq:2'}, and $|\mu_n^j| \le L$ for $n\in[k_1-j,k_2-j]$. Hence, for all $j\in\mathbb{Z}$ and $n+j\in[k_1,k_2-1]$, \eqref{eq:2} holds, which means that $
u_n^k$ defined by \eqref{eq:1.5} satisfies \eqref{eq:1-inf}. Hence, the operator $\mathfrak{A}$ has the finite Lipschitz shadowing property with constant $L$. According to Theorem ef{thm:Bernandes-shadowing}, this implies that $\mathfrak{A}$ has the Lipschitz shadowing property with constant $2L$. Let us now show that the sequence $\mathcal{A}$ satisfies the bounded solution property for $I = \mathbb{Z}$. Indeed, fix $\{w_k \in \mathbb{B}\}$ with $|w_k| \leq 1$. Consider the sequence $\xi^k \in \mathfrak{X}$ defined as $$ \xi^k = \{ \dots, w_{k+1}, w_{k+1}, w_{k+1}, \dots\}, \; \xi^k_n = w_{k+1}, \quad n\in \mathbb{Z}. $$ Note that $|\xi^k| = |w_{k+1}| \leq 1$. Since $\mathfrak{A}$ has the shadowing property, there exists a sequence $
u^k \in \mathfrak{X}$ with $|
u^k| \leq 2L$ such that  egin{equation} 
u^{k+1}=\mathfrak{A}
u^k+\xi^k,\quad k \in \mathbb{Z}. \end{equation} Denote $
u^k = \{
u^k_n\}$, then $|
u^k_n| \leq 2L$. According to \eqref{eq:nu-inf}, the sequence $v_k = 
u^k_k$ satisfies \eqref{eq:Inh} for $I = \mathbb{Z}$ and hence $\mathcal{A}$ satisfies the bounded solution property with constant $2L$ for $I = \mathbb{Z}$.

The proof uses ideas similar to those in Lemma ef{lem:CL-robust-Ak}. We highlight the main differences. First, we make a construction of $	ilde{E}^s_x$. Fix $\varepsilon >0$, $\lambda_1 \in (\lambda, 1)$. Denote $F_x = A(x)^{-1}E^u_{\alpha(x)}$  egin{minipage}{0.49	extwidth}  egin{align*} A_x^{ss}: E_x^s 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad A_x^{ss} = P_{\alpha(x)}A(x),\ A_x^{su}: E_x^u 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad A_x^{su} = P_{\alpha(x)}A(x),\ A_x^{us}: E_x^s 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad A_x^{us} = Q_{\alpha(x)}A(x),\ A_x^{uu}: E_x^u 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad A_x^{uu} = Q_{\alpha(x)}A(x), \end{align*} \end{minipage} \hfill  egin{minipage}{0.49	extwidth}  egin{align*} B_x^{ss}: E_x^s 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad B_x^{ss} = P_{ eta(x)}A(x),\ B_x^{su}: E_x^u 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad B_x^{su} = P_{ eta(x)}A(x),\ B_x^{us}: E_x^s 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad B_x^{us} = Q_{ eta(x)}A(x),\ B_x^{uu}: E_x^u 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad B_x^{uu} = Q_{ eta(x)}A(x), \end{align*} \end{minipage} \smallskip According to (C-GH2)  egin{equation} A^{us}_x = 0. \end{equation} Since $P_x, Q_x$ depend uniformly continuously on $x$, for any $\varepsilon_1 \in (0, 1)$ there exists $\delta > 0$ such that, if $ eta$ satisfies \eqref{eq:al-be-ep}, then  egin{equation} \|A_x^{ss} - B_x^{ss}\|, \|A_x^{us} - B_x^{us}\|,\|A_x^{su} - B_x^{su}\|,\|A_x^{uu} - B_x^{uu}\| < \varepsilon_1, \quad x \in \mathbb{B}. \end{equation} Define $J_x: E^u_{\alpha(x)} 	o E^u_{ eta(x)}$ as $J_x := Q_{ eta(x)}|_{E^u_{\alpha(x)}}$, then $J_x$ is an isomorphism, and  egin{equation}
otag |J_x v_1 - v_1| \leq \varepsilon_1 |v_1|, \; |J^{-1}_x v_2 - v_2| \leq \varepsilon_1 |v_2|, \quad x \in \mathbb{B}, v_1 \in E^u_{\alpha(x)}, v_2 \in E^u_{ eta(x)}. \end{equation} In the following we show that for any $\lambda_2 \in (\lambda, \lambda_1)$ there exists $C_2 > 0$ and small enough~$\varepsilon_1$ such that $B^{ss}$ and $B^{uu}$ satisfy  egin{itemize} \item[	extbf{(B1)}] $ \|B^{ss}_{ eta^{n-1}(x)} \dots B^{ss}_{ eta(x)}B^{ss}_x\| \leq C_2\lambda_2^n, \quad n \geq 0, x \in \mathbb{B}, $ \item[	extbf{(B2)}] $B^{uu}_{x}$ has the right inverse $Z_x: E_{ eta(x)}^u 	o F_x$ such that  egin{equation} B^{uu}_{x} Z_x = Id|_{E_{ eta(x)}^u}, \|Z_x\| \leq (1+\varepsilon_1)R, \end{equation}  egin{equation} \|Z_{ eta^{-n}(x)} \dots Z_{ eta^{-2}(x)}Z_{ eta^{-1}(x)}\| \leq C_2\lambda_2^n, \quad n \geq 0, x \in \mathbb{B}. \end{equation} \end{itemize} The proof of 	extbf{(B1)} is similar to the argument in \eqref{eq:GH3Bs}. Fix $\lambda_2$ and choose $N$ so that $2C\lambda^N \leq \lambda_2^N$. Set $C_2=(R(C+2)/\lambda_2)^N$. Note that for any $\delta_1 > 0$ there exists $\delta > 0$ such that, whenever $ eta$ satisfies \eqref{eq:al-be-ep}, it holds $ \mathrm{dist}( eta^n(x), \alpha^n(x)) \leq \delta_1 $ , $(n \in [1, N])$. For small enough~$\delta_1$ we have $$ \|B^{ss}_{ eta^{N-1}(x)} \dots B^{ss}_{ eta(x)}B^{ss}_x\| \leq 2\|A^{ss}_{\alpha^{N-1}(x)} \dots A^{ss}_{\alpha(x)}A^{ss}_x\|. $$ Similarly to \eqref{eq:B-proof} we conclude 	extbf{(B1)}. The proof of 	extbf{(B2)} is similar to the argument in 	extbf{(B1)} and \eqref{eq:GH3Bs-inv}. Choosing $N$ as in 	extbf{(B1)}, reducing additionally $\delta_1$ we may assume that $ \mathrm{dist}( eta^{-n}(x), \alpha^{-n}(x)) \leq \delta_1 $, $(n \in [1, N])$. Define $Z_x = A(x)^{-1}J_x^{-1}$, which satisfies \eqref{eq:Zx-cond}. As in 	extbf{(B1)}, we conclude that $$ \|Z_{ eta^{-N}(x)} \dots Z_{ eta^{-2}(x)}Z_{ eta^{-1}(x)}\| \leq 2 \|A^{-1}_{\alpha^{-N}(x)} \dots A^{-1}_{\alpha^{-2}(x)}A^{-1}_{\alpha^{-1}(x)}|_{E_x^u}\| \leq 2C\lambda^N \leq \lambda_2^N. $$ For arbitrary $n\geq0$, write $n=lN+r$, where $l\geq0$ and $0\leq r<N$. Splitting the product into blocks of length $N$ and using $\|Z_x\|\leq(1+\varepsilon_1)R$, we obtain \eqref{eq:Zx-exp}. Item 	extbf{(B2)} is proved. In the following, we construct a map $H_x^s:E_x^s 	o F_x$ with the following property: for any $v^s \in E^s_x$ there exists $w^s \in E_{ eta(x)}^s$ such that  egin{equation} A(x)(v^s+ H^s_xv^s) = w^s+ H^s_{ eta(x)}(w^s) \end{equation} For an arbitrary map $H_x^s$ we can represent $A(x)(v^s+ H^s_x(v^s)) = w^s + w^u$, where  egin{equation*} w^s = B^{ss}_x v^s + B^{su}_x H^s_xv^s \in E^s_{ eta(x)}, \quad w^u = B^{us}_x v^s + B^{uu}_x H^s_xv^s \in E^u_{ eta(x)}. \end{equation*} Condition \eqref{eq:Hsx-cond1} is equivalent to $w^u = H^s_{ eta(x)}w^s$, i.e.,  egin{equation} B^{uu}_x H^s_x = H_{ eta(x)}^s(B^{ss}_x + B^{su}_xH^s_x) - B^{us}_x. \end{equation} To satisfy \eqref{eq:H-via-B} it is sufficient to verify the following (compare with \eqref{eq:StHk})  egin{equation} H_x^s = Z_x H^s_{ eta(x)} B_x^{ss} + (Z_x H^s_{ eta(x)}B^{su}_xH^s_x - Z_xB^{us}_x). \end{equation} As in Section ef{sec:robust-Ak}, we consider the following auxiliary problem. Given a family of bounded linear operators $S_x:E^s_x 	o F_x$ that depends uniformly continuously on $x$, we seek a family of bounded linear operators $H_x: E^s_x 	o F_x$ that depends uniformly continuously on $x$, which satisfies $ H_x = Z_xH_{ eta(x)}B_x^{ss} + S_x $ (compare to \eqref{eq:HkSk}). Denote by $U$ the Banach space of uniformly bounded linear operators $S_x : E^s_x 	o F_x$ that depend uniformly continuously on $x$. Define an operator $F: U 	o U$ by $F(S_x) = H_x$, where  egin{equation}
otag H_x = S_x + \sum_{l = 1}^{+\infty}Z_x\cdot \ldots \cdot Z_{ eta^{l-1}(x)}S_{ eta^{l}(x)}B_{ eta^{l-1}(x)}^{ss} \cdot \ldots \cdot B_x^{ss}. \end{equation} Similarly to \eqref{eq:FL'} the following holds  egin{equation} \|F(S_x)\| \leq \|S_x\| \left(1+ \sum_{l=1}^{\infty} C_2^2\lambda_2^{2l} ight) = \|S_x\|\left(1+ C_2^2  rac{\lambda_2^2}{1-\lambda_2^2}ight) = L'_2 \|S_x\|. \end{equation} Since $ eta$ and $ eta^{-1}$ are uniformly continuous, the right-hand side depends uniformly continuously on $x$, and hence $H_x \in U$. Define the nonlinear operator $G:U 	o U$ by $G(H_x) = S_x$, where $$ S_x = Z_x H^s_{ eta(x)}B^{su}_xH^s_x - Z_x B^{us}_x. $$ Similarly to Section ef{sec:robust-Ak}, for small enough $\delta > 0$, $\varepsilon_2 > 0$ and $\|H_x\|, \|H'_x\| \leq \varepsilon_2$ we have $ \|GH_x-GH'_x\| \leq  rac{1}{2L_2'}\|H_x-H'_x\|. $ Since $F$ is linear, inequality \eqref{eq:FL'cont} implies that  egin{equation}
otag \|F \circ GH - F \circ GH'\| \leq L_2' rac{1}{2L_2'}\|H-H'\| =  rac{1}{2}\|H-H'\|, \end{equation} According to \eqref{eq:Asux}, \eqref{eq:AB-ep1} and 	extbf{(B2)} we have inequalities $\|G(H_x = 0)\| = \|Z_xB^{us}_x\| \leq CR\varepsilon_1$ and $ \|F \circ G (H_x = 0)\|\leq L_2'CR\varepsilon_1 $. Hence, there exists $H_x \in U$ with $\|H_x\| \leq 2L_2'CR\varepsilon_1$ such that $F\circ G (H_x) = H_x$, which implies \eqref{eq:H-via-Z}. Consequently, \eqref{eq:H-via-B} holds. Proceeding as in \eqref{eq:B-proof}, by choosing $\varepsilon_1$ sufficiently small and, if necessary, decreasing $\delta$, we obtain generalized hyperbolicity estimates with exponent $\lambda_1$ for spaces $	ilde{E}_x^s$. \medskip Now we carry out the corresponding construction for $	ilde{E}^u_x$. Here, the stable and unstable cases are genuinely asymmetric, more so than in Lemma~ef{lem:CL-robust-Ak}: there the operators are perturbed while the index shift $k 	o k+1$ is common to $\mathcal{A}$ and its perturbation, whereas here the cocycle $A$ is fixed and the base map is perturbed, $\alpha 	o  eta$. Time-reversal $A 	o A^{-1}$ therefore does not turn the perturbed system into a base-only perturbation of the reversed original (its fiber map is sampled at $ eta^{-1}(x)$, not $\alpha^{-1}(x)$), so $H^s_x$ and $H^u_x$ act between genuinely different spaces and cannot be related by symmetry; this is what forces the auxiliary splitting at $\alpha( eta^{-1}(x))$ introduced below. In order to define $H^u_{x}: E_{\alpha(x)}^u 	o A(x)E_x^s$ we need to establish an analog of \eqref{eq:Hsx-cond1}, i.e. to show that for any $	ilde{v}^u \in 	ilde{E}^u_{ eta(x)}$ there exists $	ilde{w}^u \in 	ilde{E}^u_{x}$ such that $ A(x)	ilde{w}^u = 	ilde{v}^u $, equivalently, for any $v^u \in E^u_{\alpha(x)}$ there exists $w^u \in E^u_{\alpha( eta^{-1}(x))}$ such that  egin{equation} w^u + H_{ eta^{-1}(x)}^uw^u = A^{-1}(x)(v^u + H_x^uv^u). \end{equation} Note that $$ H_{ eta^{-1}(x)}^uw^u \in A( eta^{-1}(x))E^s_{ eta^{-1}(x)} \subset E^s_{\alpha( eta^{-1}(x))}, \quad H_{x}^uv^u \in A(x)E^s_{x} \subset E^s_{\alpha(x)}. $$ For small enough $\delta$ we have the splitting $\mathbb{B} = E^s_{\alpha( eta^{-1}(x))} \oplus E^u_{\alpha( eta^{-1}(x))}$. Denote by $	ilde{P}_x$, $	ilde{Q}_x$ the projections corresponding to this splitting. Similarly to the construction of $	ilde{E}^s_x$ define  egin{align*} {C}_x^{ss}: E_{\alpha(x)}^s 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad C_x^{ss} = 	ilde{P}_{x}A^{-1}(x); &\qquad {C}_x^{su}: E_{\alpha(x)}^u 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad C_x^{su} = 	ilde{P}_{x}A^{-1}(x);\ {C}_x^{us}: E_{\alpha(x)}^s 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad C_x^{us} = 	ilde{Q}_{x}A^{-1}(x); & \qquad {C}_x^{uu}: E_{\alpha(x)}^u 	o \mathbb{B}, & \quad C_x^{uu} = 	ilde{Q}_{x}A^{-1}(x). \end{align*} Note that since we considered the decomposition of $A^{-1}$, the operator $C_x^{ss}$ is ``expanding'' and the operator $C_x^{uu}$ is ``contracting''. Equation \eqref{eq:cond-Eu} is equivalent to $$ w^u = {C}^{uu}_x v^u + C^{us}_x H_{x}^uv^u, \quad H_{ eta^{-1}(x)}^uw^u = {C}^{su}_x v^u + {C}^{ss}_x H_{x}^uv^u. $$ Hence, the condition on $H^u$ is  egin{equation} H_{ eta^{-1}(x)}^u \circ \left( {C}^{uu}_x + C^{us}_x H_{x}^u ight) = {C}^{su}_x + {C}^{ss}_x H_{x}^u. \end{equation} Similarly to \eqref{eq:Zx-cond} define the operator $	ilde{Z}_x:E^s_{\alpha( eta^{-1}(x))} 	o A(x)E^s_x$ such that $	ilde{Z}_x \circ C^{ss}_x = Id_{A(x)E^s_x}$. Condition \eqref{eq:H-Eu-cond-1} is equivalent to  egin{equation} H_{x}^u = 	ilde{Z}_x H_{ eta^{-1}(x)}^u {C}^{uu}_x + 	ilde{Z}_x H_{ eta^{-1}(x)}^u C^{us}_x H_{x}^u -	ilde{Z}_x C^{su}_x. \end{equation} Exactly as for 	extbf{(B1)} and \eqref{eq:Zx-exp}, but using (C-GH3) for $A^{-1}$ in place of the estimates for $A$, for small enough $\varepsilon_1$ and some $C_2 > 0$ the contracting block $C^{uu}$ and the right inverse $	ilde{Z}_x$ satisfy  egin{equation}
otag \|C^{uu}_{ eta^{-(n-1)}(x)} \cdots C^{uu}_{ eta^{-1}(x)}C^{uu}_x\| \leq C_2\lambda_2^n, \quad \|	ilde{Z}_x 	ilde{Z}_{ eta^{-1}(x)} \cdots 	ilde{Z}_{ eta^{-(n-1)}(x)}\| \leq C_2\lambda_2^n, \quad n \geq 0,\ x \in \mathbb{B}. \end{equation} Hence \eqref{eq:H-Eu-cond-2} is the analog of \eqref{eq:H-via-Z} with $ eta, B^{ss}_x, B^{su}_x, B^{us}_x, Z_x$ replaced by $ eta^{-1}, C^{uu}_x, C^{us}_x, C^{su}_x, 	ilde{Z}_x$, and the fixed-point argument for $H^s_x$ applies verbatim: for any $\varepsilon > 0$ and small enough $\delta$ there is an operator $H^u_x$ satisfying \eqref{eq:H-Eu-cond-2} with $\|H^u_x\| < \varepsilon$. The spaces $	ilde{E}^u_x$ satisfy the exponential estimates with exponent $\lambda_1$ as for $	ilde{E}^s_x$, via \eqref{eq:B-proof}. As in the construction of $H^s_x$ we prove that $H^u_x$ depends uniformly continuously on~$x$. Let us prove equality \eqref{eq:tildeE-EF}. According to construction $	ilde{E}^s_x \subset E^s_x \oplus F_x$ and  egin{align*} A^{-1}(x)	ilde{E}^u_{ eta(x)} & \subset A^{-1}(x)E^u_{\alpha(x)} \oplus A^{-1}(x)A(x)E^s_x = E^s_x \oplus F_x. \end{align*} Hence,  egin{equation} 	ilde{E}^s_x \oplus A^{-1}(x)	ilde{E}^u_{ eta(x)} \subset E^s_x \oplus F_x. \end{equation} For any $x \in \mathbb{B}$ and $v^s_x \in E^s_x$ define the vectors $$ v^u_x := H^s_xv^s_x \in F_x = A^{-1}(x)E^u_{\alpha(x)}, \quad v^u_{\alpha(x)} := A(x)v^u_x \in E^u_{\alpha(x)}, \quad v^s_{\alpha(x)} := H^u_x v^u_{\alpha(x)} \in A(x)E_x^s. $$ Then $v^s_x + v^u_x \in 	ilde{E}^s_x$ and $v^u_{\alpha(x)} + v^s_{\alpha(x)} \in 	ilde{E}^u_{ eta(x)}$. Hence, $$ v^s_x - A^{-1}(x) H^u_x A(x) H^s_x v^s_x = v^s_x + v^u_x - A^{-1}(x)(v^u_{\alpha(x)} + v^s_{\alpha(x)}) \in 	ilde{E}^s_x \oplus A^{-1}(x)	ilde{E}^u_{ eta(x)}. $$ Note that for $\varepsilon < 1/R$ the following holds $$ A^{-1}(x) H^u_x A(x) H^s_x (E^s_x) \subset E^s_x, \quad \|A^{-1}(x) H^u_x A(x) H^s_x\| < 1, $$ hence $Id - A^{-1}(x) H^u_x A(x) H^s_x$ is bijective on $E^s_x$. In particular $ E^s_x \subset 	ilde{E}^s_x \oplus A^{-1}(x)	ilde{E}^u_{ eta(x)} $. Similarly, we show $ F_x = A^{-1}(x)E^u_{\alpha(x)} \subset 	ilde{E}^s_x \oplus A^{-1}(x)	ilde{E}^u_{ eta(x)} $, and hence $ E^s_x \oplus F_x \subset 	ilde{E}^s_x \oplus A^{-1}(x)	ilde{E}^u_{ eta(x)} $, which together with inclusion \eqref{eq:tildeE-EF-1} implies \eqref{eq:tildeE-EF}.

Let us prove that under the assumptions of Lemma ef{lem:Bk-Inf}, the operator $\mathfrak{A}$ has the finite shadowing property. Consider a finite interval $[k_1, k_2]$ and sequences $\xi^k\in \mathfrak{X}$, $k \in [k_1, k_2]$, with $|\xi^k|\leq1$; denote by $\xi_n^k \in \mathbb{B}$ ($k\in[k_1,k_2]$, $n\in\mathbb{Z}$) their elements. Below we show that there exists a sequence $
u^k \in \mathfrak{X}$, $k\in[k_1,k_2]$ with $|
u^k| \leq L$ such that  egin{equation} 
u^{k+1}=\mathfrak{A}
u^k+\xi^k,\quad k \in [k_1, k_2-1]. \end{equation} Denote the elements of $
u^k$ by $
u^k_n \in \mathbb{B}$, i.e. $
u^k = \{
u^k_n\}$. Equations \eqref{eq:nu} are equivalent to  egin{equation} 
u_{n+1}^{k+1}=A_n 
u_n^k+\xi_{n+1}^k, \qquad n \in \mathbb{Z},\; k \in [k_1,k_2-1]. \end{equation} The idea is to read the doubly-indexed system \eqref{eq:1-inf} along the diagonals $k-n=j$: on each of them it becomes a finite inhomogeneous difference equation in the single index $n$ for the operators $\{A_n\}$, to which the finite bounded solution property applies. Denote $j := k-n$ and consider the sequences  egin{equation} \mu_n^j := 
u_n^{j+n},\qquad 	heta_n^j := \xi_n^{j+n-1}. \end{equation} Then \eqref{eq:1-inf} is equivalent to  egin{equation} \mu_{n+1}^j = A_n\mu_n^j+	heta_{n+1}^j,\qquad n\in\mathbb{Z},\; n+j\in[k_1,k_2-1]. \end{equation} Rewriting indexes we have  egin{equation} \mu_{n+1}^j = A_n\mu_n^j+	heta_{n+1}^j,\qquad j\in\mathbb{Z},\; n\in[k_1-j,\,k_2-j-1]. \end{equation} For any $j\in\mathbb{Z}$, according to \eqref{eq:Inh} there exists a sequence $\mu^j_n$ satisfying \eqref{eq:2'}, and $|\mu_n^j| \le L$ for $n\in[k_1-j,k_2-j]$. Hence, for all $j\in\mathbb{Z}$ and $n+j\in[k_1,k_2-1]$, \eqref{eq:2} holds, which means that $
u_n^k$ defined by \eqref{eq:1.5} satisfies \eqref{eq:1-inf}. Hence, the operator $\mathfrak{A}$ has the finite Lipschitz shadowing property with constant $L$. According to Theorem ef{thm:Bernandes-shadowing}, this implies that $\mathfrak{A}$ has the Lipschitz shadowing property with constant $2L$.

Consider a finite interval $[k_1, k_2]$ and sequences $\xi^k\in \mathfrak{X}$, $k \in [k_1, k_2]$, with $|\xi^k|\leq1$; denote by $\xi_n^k \in \mathbb{B}$ ($k\in[k_1,k_2]$, $n\in\mathbb{Z}$) their elements. Below we show that there exists a sequence $
u^k \in \mathfrak{X}$, $k\in[k_1,k_2]$ with $|
u^k| \leq L$ such that  egin{equation} 
u^{k+1}=\mathfrak{A}
u^k+\xi^k,\quad k \in [k_1, k_2-1]. \end{equation} Denote the elements of $
u^k$ by $
u^k_n \in \mathbb{B}$, i.e. $
u^k = \{
u^k_n\}$. Equations \eqref{eq:nu} are equivalent to  egin{equation} 
u_{n+1}^{k+1}=A_n 
u_n^k+\xi_{n+1}^k, \qquad n \in \mathbb{Z},\; k \in [k_1,k_2-1]. \end{equation}

Since $\mathfrak{A}$ has the shadowing property, there exists a sequence $
u^k \in \mathfrak{X}$ with $|
u^k| \leq 2L$ such that  egin{equation} 
u^{k+1}=\mathfrak{A}
u^k+\xi^k,\quad k \in \mathbb{Z}. \end{equation}
